Then in 1994, another old-fashioned baseball only ballpark called Jacob's Field opened in downtown Cleveland, and that was just the beginning. It became impossible to build a new ballpark and not have it look like an old ballpark. That's Mark Lampster, architecture critic at the Dallas Morning News, and a fellow at the Harvard Graduate School of Design. And that way I'd like to joke that baseball owners were a bit like teenagers.
然后在 1994 年，另一座名为雅各布斯球场（Jacob's Field）的复古纯棒球场在克利夫兰市中心开业，而这仅仅是个开始。建造一座新球场却不让它看起来像个老球场，这变得不可能了。这是马克·兰普斯特（Mark Lamster），《达拉斯晨报》（Dallas Morning News）的建筑评论员，也是哈佛大学设计研究生院的研究员。我喜欢开玩笑说，棒球老板们在某种程度上就像青少年。

24
00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:51.000
What the first cool one does, then all of a sudden, everybody else does. So if one person has a retro ballpark and it's successful then the conventional wisdom becomes in order to be successful you have to have a retro ballpark. In the 25 years since Camden opened there have been 20 new stadiums built and there's not a concrete donut in the bunch. And just like Camden Yards, most of these new stadiums have been built close to city centers, and all but one of them have been paid for, at least in part, with public money.
第一个酷的人做了什么，突然之间，其他人都会跟着做。所以，如果一个人拥有一个复古球场并且很成功，那么传统观点就会变成：要想成功，你也必须拥有一个复古球场。在卡姆登开业以来的 25 年里，已经建造了 20 座新球场，其中没有一个是什么混凝土甜甜圈。就像金莺公园一样，这些新球场大多建在靠近市中心的地方，而且除了其中一座之外，其余所有球场至少有一部分是用公共资金支付的。

25
00:13:51.000 --> 00:14:16.000
Camden Yards really hit upon the formula, right? This is Neil Demas, a journalist who studies stadium economics. Here's something that's supposedly a win-win-win, right? It's a win for the team because they get new revenue, it's a win for the fans because they get a stadium that they love, and it's a win for the team because they get new revenue. It's a win for the fans because they get a stadium that they love. And it's a win for the city because they get to revitalize a district. But most economists agree that if you want to revitalize a neighborhood, there are plenty of better ways than building a ballpark.
金莺公园确实找到了秘诀，对吧？这是尼尔·德马斯（Neil Demas），一位研究体育场经济学的记者。这里有一些被认为是双赢赢的事，对吧？这对球队来说是双赢，因为他们获得了新的收入；对球迷来说是双赢，因为他们得到了一个他们热爱的体育场；对球队来说是双赢，因为他们获得了新的收入。这对球迷来说是双赢，因为他们得到了他们热爱的体育场。这对城市来说是双赢，因为他们得以重振一个街区。但大多数经济学家都认为，如果你想重振一个街区，比起建造一座球场，有的是更好的方法。

26
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:46.000
Demas says that when a new stadium gets built, you'll see some sports bars pop up nearby. But most businesses can't rely on baseball crowds as a customer base. You know, the 81 games a year, that means there's what, like, you know, 280 days a year when there's nothing going on there. That's an awful lot of non-activity that you have to make up for. There's also the more fundamental question of whether the public should have to pay for privately owned buildings. Mark Lampster says it's tricky. Sports teams occupy
